+**Dayton termination ownership (BC03):** The horizon-triggered `pending_dayton` decision
+remains the packaged player's negotiation route. `resolve-dayton` resolves that menu through
+`resolveDaytonNegotiation`, which records `dayton_result`, sets the terminal outcome and freezes
+the complete endgame snapshot. The narrated `dayton_signed` event flag does not terminate the
+turn pipeline or replace the player's negotiation. The signing narrative requires the existing RBiH accepted-talks flag; rejecting those talks suppresses that narrative while leaving the horizon negotiation reachable. See the [bounded BC03 evidence](../plans/2026-07-31-full-campaign-electron-validation-plan.md#bc03-bounded-implementation--2026-09-07).
+
 **State contract (current player shell):** The same serialized `GameState` is pushed to all renderers via `game-state-updated`. The raw payload may still contain compatibility-era state such as `assignable_front_segments`, `brigade_front_assignment`, `brigade_sector_override`, `theatres`, and `army_theatre_assignment`, but the live tactical-map `LoadedGameState` treats those as compatibility/history or read-only assignment context rather than active presidential controls. The current player shell centers on canonical front edges, corps sectors, presidential directives, and `military.campaign_plans` (read-only; CampaignPlan objects produced by Army HQ Gathering — see `army_hq_gathering.ts`). Electron's canonical save load preserves persisted sectors and recomputes the transient `sector_combat_ratings` cache before runtime serialization; canonical disk serialization still omits the cache, and player projection retains only the player's own sector-rating keys. See [TACTICAL_MAP_SYSTEM.md](TACTICAL_MAP_SYSTEM.md) §10.4 for current single-source notes.
 
 **Derived adapter fields (not IPC channels):** `GameStateAdapter.ts` derives additional view-model fields from the raw `GameState` payload. Notable: `sectorIntel` (`SectorIntelRecordView[]`) — derived from `state.sector_intel` and `state.military.corps_front_sectors` in a single merged pass (also produces `fogOfWar`). The live player DTO is intentionally reduced: it carries only the friendly-sector threat summary fields the Army HQ shell actually renders (`friendly_sector_id`, `enemy_sector_id`, strength category, posture, offensive signs, confidence, turns-in-contact, visible brigades, front-edge count). Raw enemy faction/corps identity stays engine-side unless and until a player-safe shell explicitly needs it. No IPC round-trip — entirely client-side derivation from the `game-state-updated` payload.
